<TopicPage slug="subjects/real-estate/foundational/property-and-ownership">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Real estate's foundation is a legal idea: ownership of land is not one right but a
    <strong>bundle of rights</strong> — to possess, use, exclude others, transfer and encumber —
    and each right can be separated, sold or limited. What a buyer purchases, what a tenant rents
    and what a lender holds are all different bundles tied to the same dirt.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the bundle, the estates, and what title proves" widget="equationStep" config={bundleCfg} />

  <h2>Estates: ownership has a clock</h2>
  <p>
    The fee simple absolute — the full bundle, indefinitely, inheritable — is what most people
    mean by "owning" a home. But the law carves time: a life estate ends at a named person's
    death; a leasehold conveys use for years while the fee stays with the landlord. Valuation
    follows: a 99-year lease and a freehold on identical land are different assets with different
    prices.
  </p>

  <h2>Encumbrances price themselves</h2>
  <p>
    Every removed stick costs money. A utility easement across the garden, a restrictive covenant
    against outbuildings, a tax lien, a zoning line — the market reprices each. This is why title
    examination exists: tracing the chain of deeds and recorded claims so the buyer knows exactly
    which bundle, with which sticks missing, is actually changing hands.
  </p>

  <h2>Fixtures: where the categories argue</h2>
  <p>
    Is a chandelier real property or the seller's personal property? A fitted kitchen? A
    above-ground pool? Law answers with tests — attachment, adaptation, intention — and the
    answers drive contract drafting. Real estate's disputes are rarely about the land itself;
    they are about which bundle was promised.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> national licensing exam content outline (ARELLO-aligned); Reilly, <em>The Language of Real Estate</em>;property-law treatises on estates and fixtures.</p>
</TopicPage>
